Title: Fix guard-commit-on-main.sh false-negative: check tree identity, not branch name
Created: 2026-07-13
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-07-13T07:00Z claude created child of 0252 (author ratified model A, 2026-07-13)

--- body ---
## Context

Child of tracking ticket 0252. The 0252 audit
(docs/audit-0252-worktree-ownership-2026-07-12.md) found that
`guard-commit-on-main.sh` checks only `branch == main`. In incident I1 the
stray `git checkout -B` moved the PRIMARY checkout onto a feature branch,
after which the guard happily allowed commits on the primary — the exact
damage it exists to prevent. The material predicate is "this commit lands
on the primary checkout", not "the branch is named main".

## Relevant files

- `scripts/guard-commit-on-main.sh` — the guard to fix
- `scripts/guard-cd-primary-repo.sh` — sibling guard that already consumes `.cwd` from hook JSON; copy its input handling

## Actions

1. Add a tree-identity check: resolve the command's effective cwd
   (consume `.cwd` from the PreToolUse hook JSON like the sibling guard,
   instead of trusting the shell's inherited cwd) and block commits whose
   `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` is the primary checkout root, whatever
   the branch name.
2. Keep the existing branch==main check as a second layer.

## Test

- Red first: primary checkout sitting on a non-main branch, hook JSON cwd
  pointing at it — assert the guard blocks the commit (today it passes).

## Verification

- [ ] Commit on primary checkout blocked regardless of branch name
- [ ] Commit in a registered worktree on a feature branch still allowed
- [ ] Guard reads cwd from hook JSON, not from its own process cwd

## Invariants

- `make check` passes; no false positives on normal worktree commits

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Both checks live in the guard with tests proving RED on the pre-fix state
- [ ] `make check` passes
